WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:05.920
MICHIEL VANTHOOR:
Hey, everybody.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.530
How is it going?

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.680
Had a good lunch?

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.100
So nice sandwiches, good
coffee, good drinks?

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.895
All set to go for another couple
of hours of awesome Google

00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:18.170
Cloud technology.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.390
So today, in my
session, I'm going

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.070
to talk to you about building
a custom chatbot with API.AI

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.310
and Cloud Functions.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.860
So who has ever built
a chatbot before?

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:29.800
OK.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.170
Who is planning to build one in
the next, let's say, 12 months?

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.230
Ah.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.440
Obviously, chatbots are a
little bit the hype right now.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.190
So I'm very glad that we had
this big room to go through it

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:43.390
with us together.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.070
So my name is Michiel Vanthoor.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.070
I'm a customer
engineer at Google,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.810
based in Google Amsterdam.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.010
And in this session, we
will cover three things--

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.580
the first one is the
basic building blocks

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.210
of building a chatbot.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.750
The second one is
a demonstration

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.580
of how these building
blocks actually work.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.550
So this will be the
exciting part for me.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.349
And hopefully, also
together with you,

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:06.950
we can have some
working prototype

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.500
by the end of the session.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.070
And then, last but not
least, Tim Daniels from ING

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.980
will share what they have
built the last six months

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.190
and also the lessons
learned along the way.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.490
But, first, maybe it's a good
idea to take a step back.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.220
So the people who were in
the keynote this morning

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.560
have already heard our
mission statement--

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.450
"To organize the
world's information

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.760
and make it universal,
accessible, and useful."

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.430
And if you think
about it, this is also

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.170
what we're doing
for Google Cloud.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.636
So we're organizing your
business information

00:01:37.636 --> 00:01:39.010
and make the
information you want

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.190
to share universally
accessible and useful

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.790
for your stakeholders, your
customers, your partners,

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:49.120
and also your employees.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.770
What's maybe
interesting about that

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.480
is that we have
developed a lot of tools

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.860
for organizing information.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.210
We have covered big data.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.080
We're looking into
machine learning.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.400
But the part I want to focus
on is making it universally

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.840
accessible.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.020
This not only means making it
accessible across the globe

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.230
with low latency and
high availability,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.430
but it is also mentioning
how people actually

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.610
interact with your information.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.530
And that's where we
see a lot of change

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.690
happening the last few years.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.350
So, traditionally, people
had to adjust to technology

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:21.170
to cope with it.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.560
So people had to learn
to work with a keyboard.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.880
People had to learn to work
with a mouse, stuff like that.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.370
You can even go back to the
typewriter, where people also

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.240
had to learn to work with typing
and not writing or speaking--

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.110
the natural way of
people to communicate.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.760
Obviously, around
2008, there was

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.820
this smartphone with this
giant touchscreen, which

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.870
enabled you to use gestures, to
actually use your fingers-- you

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:51.400
know, these tools
you use everyday--

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.130
to interact with the
world around you,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.810
to also get something
back from technology.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.080
And now, the third wave we're
seeing in this evolution

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.530
is the AI [INAUDIBLE] so that we
really can talk to the computer

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.290
like we do.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.830
So the humans don't have
to adjust to technology,

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.270
but technology can
adjust to humans.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.510
So we can just speak
like we normally do.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.780
We can just write like a
normal written sentence

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.850
to get something
back from technology.

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:19.300
And this is obviously
where the chatbots fit in.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.880
Through the advancement in
machine learning and compute,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.370
we are now at a stage where
we are very confident in what

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.570
the technology can do.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.760
And that's also why you
see a lot of activity

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.750
around this topic.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.790
So let's make it a
little bit more concrete

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.030
and look at the
basic architecture.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.580
This architecture is
both for voice chatbots--

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.450
so you speak to them,
like the Amazon Alexa,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.030
Microsoft Cortana, or, of
course, the Google Home--

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.780
but can also be
used for textbots.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.960
So if you have it
integrated on your website

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.550
or in your mobile
application, you

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.840
can use the same
basic architecture.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.640
So the first one is
the user interface.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.120
Then we have the
conversational agent platform.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.370
And to fill it up, we
have the fulfillment side.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.330
So we will go over
them one by one.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.800
And later on I will also
show you how they work.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.760
So the user interface-- this
is where your user interacts

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.420
with your bot.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.510
This is where it will
connect with your chatbot.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.610
Of course, in Google,
we like to think

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.740
that if people are
looking for information,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.019
a natural way for
them to go is Google.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.440
So we are investing a lot
in our Google Assistant.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.560
It's available on Google phones,
on our Google Home devices,

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.810
but also on smartwatches
in your car,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.080
so that you can be directly
in touch with Google.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.720
You can also connect your own
chatbot to Google Assistant.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.940
So you could say something
like, OK, Google,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.870
let me talk to my chatbot.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.820
And you will get routed to
the chatbot you've just built.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.940
Of course, we also
know that not all users

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.420
are on Google all the time.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.169
And they are also
on other platforms.

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:48.460
So we also want to offer
native integrations with that.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:52.730
So think about Slack, Facebook
Messenger, Cisco Spark,

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:56.320
Microsoft Cortana,
or also Amazon Alexa.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.750
And the third, but
not least, we also

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.317
want to have a software
development kit available so

00:05:03.317 --> 00:05:05.650
that you can also integrate
in your mobile applications,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.230
your websites, so
that you can have

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:10.540
complete flexibility on
how your users want to get

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.030
in touch with your chatbot.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.700
The second building block is the
conversational agent platform.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.570
And his job is very simple
but also very complicated.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.860
He has to understand humans.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.990
So he has to understand
something like,

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.540
can I get a pizza with
bacon and cheese--

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.990
make that a large one--
and oh, add some olives,

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.140
standard crust--

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.660
this very human way--

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.590
unstructured way-- that
people communicate.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.240
So we, as developers,
of course, we

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.640
want to have a nice,
structured JSON format

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.480
where we can act upon and we can
do some nice applications upon.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:46.564
But that's, unfortunately, not
the way most people talk to us.

00:05:46.564 --> 00:05:48.980
And that's where we have to
find this conversational agent

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:49.760
platform.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.400
And this is exactly
what API.AI does.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.120
API.AI was a company we
acquired less than one year ago.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.872
And it's one of the top used
platforms of building chatbots.

00:06:00.872 --> 00:06:02.330
Just a quick poll--
how many people

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:03.371
are familiar with API.AI?

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.980
So I think that's a nice--

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.080
it's around 30% to 40% of the
room are already aware of it.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.430
And I think if I ask this
question within one year,

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.610
it will be even more--

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:19.550
especially if you pay a lot
of attention in my session.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:20.050
OK.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.220
So we've covered the
two building blocks.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.650
Now to the fulfillment side.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.740
So once we've figured out
what the user actually wants,

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.150
we also have to make it happen.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.010
And this happens on
the fulfillment side.

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:31.210
This is your back end.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.250
This is where you
write your application,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.800
like your used to doing today.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.550
But, also, there on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.539
we have some very interesting
options to look into.

00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:42.580
So the first thing you
can do is you can build up

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.750
your own virtual machines.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.230
You can build up
your own network.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.960
You can set up
your own firewall.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.930
And you can really build your
application from the ground up,

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.480
having complete control
over your configuration

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:58.006
and your control.

00:06:58.006 --> 00:07:00.700
The next thing you can do is
you can move a little bit more

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.910
to the manager averse, to the
more agile way of working.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.050
You can use containers, where
the dependencies are already

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.040
concluded.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.000
And you can run it in a managed
version, like Google Container

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:11.740
Engine.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.190
So this is the
containers as servers.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.560
But, of course, you
can also abstract it

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.240
a little bit more
further and only

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.280
concentrate on the
application codes.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.210
Then you use platform
as a service.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.850
And this could be
App Engine, we have.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:31.570
For example, Snapchat
is using App Engine

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.030
to be globally available
for all their customers

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:36.610
across the world without
having to provision

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.160
virtual machines, provision
networks, only concentrate

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.200
on their core application.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.602
And the most abstract
thing we have right now

00:07:45.602 --> 00:07:46.810
is actually a Cloud Function.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.300
So you don't focus on
the application level,

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.610
but you focus on
the function level.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.490
And from there on, you can
sail how far you want to go.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.240
So the more you go to the
right, the more no ops you have.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.570
The more you go to
your left, the more

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:05.650
control and configuration
options you have available.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.580
The App Engines on the
Cloud Function Zone

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.580
is what we call the
server-less zone.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.830
This doesn't mean that there
are no servers available.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.446
They're just abstracted
away for you.

00:08:13.446 --> 00:08:15.070
And you don't have
to worry about them.

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:16.680
You can just focus on the code.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.370
The scaling will
come automatically.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.620
So if you have one request
or one million requests,

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:21.440
you don't care.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:22.420
It just works.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.470
And also, you only
pay for what you use.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.650
So this makes it very
easy to get started

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.370
with it, to play with it.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:31.100
And if it's a success,
you can just go with it.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:34.030
The same story we
had at the Philips U

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.919
in the keynote earlier today.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:38.760
So when we look at
the Cloud Functions,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.210
specifically, they are
very perfect positions

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:47.964
for an event-oriented
architecture.

00:08:47.964 --> 00:08:49.380
This could be
background function.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:53.650
So, for example, if you upload a
file onto Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.810
you can trigger a function.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.240
And you can, for example,
get some metadata.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.460
You can use one of these
nice machine learning

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:06.370
APIs to get some metadata about
a video, or about a picture,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.600
so that you can launch it.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.580
You can attach it
to the storage.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:12.970
This is also available
for Google Cloud Pub/Sub

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.280
and also with
Firebase integrations.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.260
The other thing you can do is
you can make an HTTP function.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.290
And then you can use
it as a web hook.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:23.170
You can then start to easily
integrate it and use it as sort

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.270
of a glue for other APIs--

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:28.990
like, for example, the G Suite
APIs, but also with API.AI.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:33.380
And this is where, of course,
the perfect fit is happening.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:34.990
So this is the
basic architecture.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:38.284
Is everyone clear with
the three building blocks?

00:09:38.284 --> 00:09:39.670
Awesome.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.050
So now I will go over
them in a demonstration.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.350
I will try to build a Google
web store from scratch.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:46.360
So bear with me.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.370
We will go into this together.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.200
And we will see
what we end up with.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.570
The things I will
use is the API.AI

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.640
as a conversational
agent platform.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.550
For the fulfillment side, we
will use a Cloud Function.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:59.020
And then, on the
user interface, we

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.180
will see how it
interacts with Slack

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:05.150
and how that works in,
hopefully, under 10 minutes.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:06.950
So let's get going.

00:10:14.405 --> 00:10:15.399
[DING-DING]

00:10:20.863 --> 00:10:21.363
OK.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.120
Bear with me.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.390
So the thing you see right
now is the API.AI interface.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:51.250
On the left side, you have
your configuration options.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.070
And on the right
side, you can directly

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.650
test it out and start
interacting with the chatbot

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:57.410
you're building.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:00.530
In API.AI there are two
very important concepts

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:01.470
to be aware of.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:02.590
The first one is intents.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.830
The second ones are entities.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:09.400
Intents are actions your people,
your users, want to execute.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:14.490
And entities are objects
your users want to act upon.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:19.940
So, in my example, I want to
make this Google web shop.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:23.090
So I'm going to make
a product entity.

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:24.680
And I'm going to
sell, hopefully,

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:26.780
some Google Pixel phones.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:29.030
And you see, you also have
the option to add synonyms.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.315
Because people use different
words for the same object.

00:11:33.315 --> 00:11:35.350
So I will also add pixel.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:38.420
I will also add
[INAUDIBLE] Google pixels,

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.490
and pixels as well.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.840
Next thing we want to sell
is, maybe, a Google Home--

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:47.010
Google Homes.

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.650
And people also call it
sometimes just a Home,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.360
and a Homes.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.220
We have a Chromecast--

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.120
Chromecasts.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.890
And last, but not
least, the Chromebook--

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.120
Chromebooks.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.210
And we've now created
our own entities.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.820
The next thing we want to
do is make a first intent.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.520
And you see there are already
two intents, default, in place.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.540
So the first one is
the welcome intent.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.290
This will be launched
when the user starts

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:18.060
to interact with your chatbot.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.270
And the second one
is a fallback intent.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:24.089
If API.AI is not able to figure
out the intent, it's a fallback

00:12:24.089 --> 00:12:26.130
and it will guide the user
to the right direction

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:29.277
by maybe asking to
repeat the sentence.

00:12:29.277 --> 00:12:31.110
So let's take a look
at the welcome content.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:35.990
And you can say something
like, user says hi, or hello.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.370
And then you can respond.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:47.170
And you say something like, hey,
welcome to this awesome event.

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:56.540
What Google products
do you want?

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:57.170
We save it.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:00.330
And now we can normally
directly test this.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:01.970
So we can say, hi.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.340
You see the default
welcome intent is launched.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:08.170
And the response is, hi,
welcome to this awesome event.

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:10.820
What Google products
do you want?

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.360
So, of course, we also want
to make our own intents.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:14.890
And if you're
running a web shop,

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:18.370
we're actually hoping that
people will buy something.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:24.380
So we can make an intent
called buy product.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:28.630
And the user could say
something like, hey,

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:35.440
can I buy five Chromebooks,
or maybe something like,

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:43.290
can I get three Chromecasts,
or maybe something like,

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:48.950
can you give me
nine Google Homes.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:54.350
And what you see is
happening is that API.AI

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:57.200
is using machine learning and
natural language processing

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.990
to figure out which
entities are in place.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.210
So it's figuring out,
ah, there's Google Home,

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:03.890
there's Chromebook,
there's Chromecast.

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:06.710
This is actually the product
entity we just created.

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:10.860
Next to that, it's also
figuring out the number.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:14.640
So we can give a response
as well, say, like, sure.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:17.490
And then we can use parameters
to refer to what the user says.

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:24.320
So we can say like,
number of product, devices

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:25.880
are on their way.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:32.310
We save it.

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:34.240
And we can directly
start to play with it.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.440
So we say, hey--

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.660
OK, the welcome
intent is launched.

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.900
Let's try to buy something.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:46.545
Can I buy four Google
Pixels, please.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.800
And what you see,
indeed, it's figuring out

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.416
the buy intent we just created.

00:14:54.416 --> 00:14:56.290
It's figuring out the
parameters, the number,

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:57.190
and the product.

00:14:57.190 --> 00:15:02.714
And it's launching the correct
answer we were hoping for.

00:15:02.714 --> 00:15:04.630
So let's make it a little
bit more interesting

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.080
and maybe add the delivery date.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.740
We call it dates.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:12.940
And what you see is that API.AI
has a lot of entity types

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:13.830
already in place.

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:14.620
So you have dates.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.020
You have geographic locations.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.730
You have time zones,
all of these things.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.230
And the one I'm interested in
right now is a date entity.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:26.530
The next thing we
can do is we can

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:31.880
make these parameters required
for the intent to be complete.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.970
So we can say, a buy
intent is only complete

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.520
if you have the number of
products, the actual product,

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.920
and also the delivery date.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:41.410
So we make it required.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:43.030
And then we can also
prompt the user.

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:44.640
If he doesn't give
enough information,

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:48.730
we can prompt
something like, sure,

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:56.880
when do you want the product
devices to be delivered?

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:03.400
We can add a response as well.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:11.500
They will be delivered on date.

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:13.370
Of course, we save
what we are doing.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.870
And let's try it again.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.020
Can-- also something
interesting,

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:19.204
just for you to know.

00:16:19.204 --> 00:16:20.870
So I trained to model
with Google Homes,

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.840
Chromecast, Chromebooks.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:25.040
And in my example I was
using the Google Pixel.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.130
So it's obviously smart
enough to figure out, OK, I'm

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:29.630
expecting an entity there.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.350
You don't have to
give him everything.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.410
This is the power of
these kind of entities.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.720
I can also now just
add another entity.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:38.260
And it will also take it into
account in all this training

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:40.340
that it's doing.

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:45.590
So let's write out-- can
I buy three Chromecasts?

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.020
OK.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.360
So you see, at first
sight-- because I'm just

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.790
beginning to train
this model-- it was not

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:01.330
able to figure it out.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:03.650
So it was asking me,
what is the product?

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:05.930
And now it's asking me,
when should it be delivered?

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:11.589
And we can say something
like next week, Monday.

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:13.220
And what you're seeing here--

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:14.390
I think it's very cool--

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:16.400
is that the system is
able to figure out,

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.619
OK, the intent is buy product.

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:20.240
The number and the
product are there.

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:23.160
And the date is an exact date
that we as developers love,

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:23.786
you know?

00:17:23.786 --> 00:17:26.119
We, as a user, we just want
to say tomorrow or next week

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:26.950
Monday.

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:28.400
We want to act in a human way.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.230
We don't want to
adjust to technology.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.460
This is the point that
I was bringing earlier.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:36.121
So, pretty cool or not?

00:17:36.121 --> 00:17:36.620
OK.

00:17:41.424 --> 00:17:42.840
Another thing we
can do-- so now I

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:46.512
showed you the conversational
agent platform, how that works.

00:17:46.512 --> 00:17:48.470
But we also want to have
this fulfillment side,

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:49.261
this back-end side.

00:17:49.261 --> 00:17:50.970
So if people actually
buy something,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.950
you also want your
ERP system to kick in.

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:55.110
And you want to really
start something going on

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.400
in your back end.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:01.020
So to show you that, I'm
going to make another intent.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.220
And the way of
linking intents is

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:08.310
with another important
concept called context.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:10.392
So if people are
buying something,

00:18:10.392 --> 00:18:12.850
there is a chance that they
also want to do something else.

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:16.640
So we can-- after an end-user
has bought a product,

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:18.930
he is in some sort of a flow.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:22.960
And we could call
this the buying mode.

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:31.310
What we can do then is we
can make another intent.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:32.950
We call it add another.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:40.380
And we can, here, insert an
input context also buying mode.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.227
So the system knows that if
they like want something,

00:18:44.227 --> 00:18:45.810
the user has bought
something, there's

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:51.090
a big chance that they also will
launch the add another intent.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:54.600
And the user could say
something like, add another,

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:57.970
or, another one, please.

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.810
We save it.

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:07.610
And this time we're not going
to put the response into API.AI

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:11.140
but we're going to use the web
hook, the fulfillment side,

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:13.940
the third building block of
my presentation earlier on.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.590
So the fulfillment is
configured over here.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:20.970
It's one web hook.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:23.640
It's my project name in
Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:27.180
And it's a Cloud Function
called fulfillment.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:29.690
I will now show
you how this looks

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:32.630
like in Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:37.710
So, basically, it's a very
short function written in OJS.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:43.420
It's getting a JSON
body from API.AI

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:46.460
and it is going to look
for the Action field.

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:49.330
And if the Action field is
something like add another,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:50.650
it will get the quantity.

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:51.870
It will add one.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:55.240
And it will respond
something like, here you go,

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.850
the new number of
devices are ordered.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:59.320
You set the HTTP header.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.240
And you set the status to 200.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:03.610
You sent a response back.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:05.710
And API.AI can
take it from there.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:16.890
So now I want to make sure
that also the Action field.

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.987
So this is actually the
field where my Cloud function

00:20:19.987 --> 00:20:20.570
is looking at.

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:24.170
So this has to be there.

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:26.270
And then we can normally
try it out again.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:30.590
Let's go to the whole flow.

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:32.560
OK.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:33.560
Welcome.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:42.165
Can I buy-- maybe we can try,
can I order five Google pixels?

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:46.070
Sure.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:49.320
When do you want the Google
Pixel devices to be delivered?

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:50.360
Today, of course.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:54.320
Shortly it will be delivered.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:57.650
And you see now,
again, the intent.

00:20:57.650 --> 00:21:00.000
API.AI was figuring
out the intent.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.150
It was figuring out
the good parameters

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:05.540
by having a conversation
with me as the end user.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:08.000
And it's also giving, now,
this context of buying mode.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.267
So API.AI is now
aware from, hey,

00:21:11.267 --> 00:21:13.100
maybe this add an another
thing is something

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:15.230
that I can expect now.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:18.830
So now, for the moment of truth,
at least two of my building

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:20.460
blocks coming together.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:21.740
So let's try to add another.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:28.744
And here you go-- six
devices are ordered.

00:21:28.744 --> 00:21:29.410
Pretty cool, no?

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:30.870
[CLAPS HAND]

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:31.440
Ah.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:38.470
I mean, it's maybe a little bit
stupid for this demonstration.

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:41.530
But if you think about
it, in just, I think,

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:45.790
less than 10 minutes, we were
able to have a fully scalable

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:47.320
chatbot working.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:49.462
Of course, it still is
only a proof of concept.

00:21:49.462 --> 00:21:51.920
But if you start thinking about
these kind of applications,

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:54.490
and you can really build
this inside your companies

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:57.610
to interact with your end
users, with your partners,

00:21:57.610 --> 00:22:00.790
but also with your
employees, for example.

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:04.570
You can really already
make a huge step forward.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:09.070
So a last thing I want to show
you is my final building block.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:12.490
This was a user interface.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:17.200
So here we have all the
native integrations available.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:21.275
We also have links to the
software development kit.

00:22:21.275 --> 00:22:23.910
Actions on Google-- I
believe that you will

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:25.720
know that this will be working.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.010
So I want to show
you today's Slack.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:31.090
Let me make sure it's turned on.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:36.480
And then we go to my
API.AI bot in Slack.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:37.700
And we basically say, hey--

00:22:40.598 --> 00:22:46.410
oh-- let's do the one thing
in IT that always works.

00:22:50.856 --> 00:22:53.270
Turning it off and
on again, of course.

00:22:56.722 --> 00:22:57.222
OK.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.896
I can also blame it
on Slack, of course.

00:23:19.896 --> 00:23:20.396
No.

00:23:31.758 --> 00:23:32.760
No.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.627
Hello?

00:23:34.627 --> 00:23:36.503
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:36.503 --> 00:23:38.830
Ah.

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:40.080
Welcome to this awesome event.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.413
What Google product do you want?

00:23:41.413 --> 00:23:42.780
Ah.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:43.530
Now we're talking.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:51.110
This one is still not
completely well-trained.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.150
We can look into
this Chromebook.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.494
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:54.494 --> 00:23:56.650
MICHIEL VANTHOOR: Ah.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:57.150
OK.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:59.500
That's-- All right.

00:24:03.388 --> 00:24:05.050
Ah, now I know.

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.390
I think I only said hello
in the training, right?

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:11.160
So when I was using this intent,
I was making this welcome

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:13.120
intent-- just for
your information--

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:16.780
I only trained it on
saying hello and hi.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.190
And I was saying, hi, right?

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:19.889
OK.

00:24:19.889 --> 00:24:21.180
I was saying hi with a capital.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:22.638
So this is where
you have to train.

00:24:22.638 --> 00:24:24.350
The model will
automatically train.

00:24:24.350 --> 00:24:28.420
So what you will do and also,
maybe ING will touch upon this.

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:29.970
This is, of course,
machine learning

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:31.010
so it learns every time.

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:32.450
So you have to train it.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.080
So you can say like, hi.

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:37.190
This should launch a
default welcome intent.

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:38.100
So you approve that.

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:39.960
And then the system is learning.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.270
And you're making your
product better and better.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:43.400
So that's where it takes
a little bit of time.

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:44.990
And that's where it's a
little bit tricky for me

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:46.670
to figure it out in 10 minutes.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:48.750
But we will get there.

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.590
I want to buy
three Google Homes.

00:24:55.401 --> 00:24:55.900
Today.

00:24:59.476 --> 00:25:00.860
Add another.

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:03.470
Who actually has a good
Google Home at home?

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:04.412
OK.

00:25:04.412 --> 00:25:06.120
So the one thing, like
once you have one,

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:07.640
you want to have
one in every room.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.672
Because you start to get used
to saying something like,

00:25:10.672 --> 00:25:12.380
turn my lights on and
turn my lights off.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.050
So that's why another one
is a good intent you maybe

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:15.620
want to have.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:19.064
And here you go-- four
devices are ordered.

00:25:19.064 --> 00:25:21.554
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:27.880
So there you go.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:32.100
I hope this was
interesting for you.

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.820
As you know, it's always tricky
to do a demo live on stage.

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:38.220
But what I did, I went
over these building blocks.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.500
I started with a
conversational agent platform,

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:44.430
showing you how you can
input unstructured human data

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:47.130
and transform it into JSON,
and act upon it with a Cloud

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:47.990
Function.

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:50.760
We did the native
integration and Slack.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:54.030
So it was only a very
short proof of concept.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:55.530
I hope you can start
to play with it

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:59.040
and show it to your bosses
and show them how cool it is.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:00.670
But if you want to
do the real work,

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:03.900
it's interesting to listen
to Tim Daniels from ING.

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.247
And they will share
their lessons learned.

00:26:06.247 --> 00:26:07.648
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:10.547 --> 00:26:11.880
TIM DANIELS: Thank you, Michiel.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.650
And hello, everybody.

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.010
My name is Tim, and
indeed, I'm working

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.560
in a small but
enthusiastic team at ING.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:22.770
And we are actively building
a chatbot for banking.

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:27.780
And what I especially liked
about the talk of Michiel

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:31.530
was that it brought back the
initial excitement we felt

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:33.930
when we started to use API.AI.

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:36.100
Now we are a couple
of months later.

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:37.290
And we still like it.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:38.130
But it has evolved.

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:40.230
So it's a kind of journey.

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:41.900
And I will take you along--

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:44.560
the next 15 minutes--
along this journey.

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:46.500
But first I would like
to spend a few minutes

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:52.320
on why this is of importance to
ING, this type of technology.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:57.480
So as we are in ING's home
town, I suppose that most of you

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:00.300
know that ING is a
global, universal bank.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:04.320
And it has been a tremendous
focus on customer experience

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:09.000
the last few years and also on,
let's say, fostering internally

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.160
a real engineering culture.

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.470
And the reason
being that bankers

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.930
need to be where
their clients are.

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:19.530
And more and more, our customers
are on social platforms

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:22.230
or on other digital ecosystems.

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:25.020
So it's a very
natural thing for ING

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:29.310
to explore the
possibilities of chatbots.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.550
And so here she is.

00:27:32.550 --> 00:27:33.660
She's called Marie.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:37.590
And she's what we call
your digital assistant.

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:40.730
And it's about six months
ago that we fired up

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.500
API.AI for the very first time.

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:47.280
And now, six months
later, Marie is

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:51.930
in production for a
well-controlled group

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:55.260
of customers and ING employees.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.960
And as she will get
better and better--

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:01.050
because you need
users to train her.

00:28:01.050 --> 00:28:03.000
So we train her,
thanks to the input

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.750
we get from the first users--

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:08.970
we will gradually increase
the group of people

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.680
that have access to Marie--

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:16.260
hopefully, until every
customer of ING can use her.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.950
Secondly, what can she do?

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:23.010
She can help customers
with questions and requests

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:25.050
about cards.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:28.390
Don't be mistaken-- she's not
a question and answer bot.

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:31.530
But she's what we would
like to call a do-bot.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:35.670
She does things for customers
like ordering a replacement

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:38.070
card, activating
a card for usage

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:42.930
abroad, resetting a pin
code-- which sometimes happens

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:46.320
after a night out, as our
people in the contact center

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:49.770
know to tell me.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:51.860
Initially, we have
spent quite some time

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.150
on deciding what the exact
scope of Marie should be.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:57.690
And now, every day, we
are happy we did so.

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:00.630
Because having a clear
scope for your bot

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:03.750
saves you a lot of energy
along the way-- energy

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:07.290
you will need to cover all
the other aspects that come

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:10.140
with building a decent chatbot.

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:11.850
Maybe a last word on why cards.

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.650
First of all, cards--

00:29:13.650 --> 00:29:16.830
it's about what you can do with
this tangible piece of plastic.

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:21.310
So in the people's heads, there
is a kind of natural boundary.

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:23.820
It's clear for people when
you talk about bank cards what

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:25.270
you're actually talking about.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:28.950
And secondly, ING's still
getting a lot of calls

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:30.930
from people who want
help with their cards,

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:34.030
like resetting this pin
card, and so on and so forth.

00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:37.880
Why we have chosen for Facebook
Messenger as user interface?

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:40.060
Well, simply because
it is popular.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.210
As you have seen
with Michiel, it's

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:47.710
very easy to deploy
other channels as well.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:50.470
Now, before I do the demo--

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:53.820
which I hope will work--

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:57.780
and then we will, after the
demo, we will also peek--

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:59.820
I will open up a
API.AI so that we

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:03.210
can peek into the brain
of Marie together.

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:06.690
And then you see, let's
say, a more extended bot

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:08.150
than the one of Michiel.

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:11.730
But before I do so, I would
like to introduce, briefly,

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.880
the overall architecture
of Marie to you.

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:22.440
And what you see is that
for the user interface--

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.340
that was simple.

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:25.560
That is just one component.

00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:27.060
It's Facebook Messenger.

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:30.000
But the conversational
agent platform-- that we

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.650
have split over two components.

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:36.240
On the one hand, API.AI, where
we do all natural language

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:37.200
processing.

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:40.560
And that is important for us
because we want business people

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.780
to change what Marie is saying.

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:45.570
And therefore, we want to
have all natural language

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:49.410
generation, all the responses
of Marie also in one place.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:51.630
And there is a second
component, which

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:54.090
is straight in the
middle, and therefore, we

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:56.070
called it the middleman.

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:59.430
And in that component we have
put some very specific logic

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:00.120
for us.

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:02.370
For example, we are at the bank.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.950
If a customer orders
a replacement card,

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:07.950
we need to make sure that we
authenticate the customer.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:10.670
And because the middleman is
in between Facebook Messenger,

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:15.220
API.AI, it knows at which point
in the conversation to start

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:16.950
the authentication flow--

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:19.860
authentication which we do
via a one-time password, which

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:21.540
we send out via Twilio.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:25.080
Then, on the mobile phone,
the customer types it in,

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:29.430
gives it to Marie, and the
person is authenticated.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:32.400
As you can see, we are
fully in the cloud.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:35.550
So everything below
this thick orange line

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:38.870
are all the back-end
systems of ING

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:44.130
at 30 years of IT history
piled up in the cellar.

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:47.320
And above is all cloud.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:49.350
Now we did it in the cloud.

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:51.750
And that is one of the
reasons that allowed

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:55.310
us to do it in six months.

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:56.610
Now don't be mistaken.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:59.970
At a bank, security,
confidentiality, GDPR--

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:01.420
it's all important.

00:32:01.420 --> 00:32:04.620
So we worked very hard
with our security officers,

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:08.100
our information and operational
risk managers, our fraud

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:13.120
experts, to make it as
secure as it needs to be.

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:17.850
So we are glad that
we did that with them.

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:19.950
And it worked out fine.

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:23.760
There are three other
components on the screen, which

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.940
is the sentiment
analysis API of Google,

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:29.130
because we're keeping
track of the sentiment

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:30.780
of a conversation.

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:32.610
Because if the
conversation gets too bad,

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:35.370
we want people in the
contact center to step in.

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:40.440
Therefore, we have the sentiment
and intervention dashboard.

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:46.110
And then, last, but not least,
Marie is talking English today.

00:32:46.110 --> 00:32:49.630
And we're looking into ways to
make her quickly, let's say,

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:50.460
a polyglot.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:53.960
Because in Belgium, you need
at least three languages--

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:55.710
four, even.

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:59.090
So we're doing some
experiments over there.

00:32:59.090 --> 00:33:00.090
OK.

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:05.690
Now I hope-- because this is a
corporate laptop and there is

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.640
a demo with real
back-end interaction--

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:10.800
I hope it is going
to work out fine.

00:33:14.209 --> 00:33:16.650
[DING-DING]

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:19.620
So can I have the
laptop on the screen?

00:33:19.620 --> 00:33:20.501
Ah.

00:33:20.501 --> 00:33:21.000
OK.

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:23.610
So you see, here she is.

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:28.050
The advantage of a bot is that
she is not nervous, like I am.

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:30.880
But, luckily, she
still remembers me.

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:32.910
Hi, Tim, she says,
which is important

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:34.710
for the personalization.

00:33:34.710 --> 00:33:36.810
And for the moment,
only English.

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:37.650
OK.

00:33:37.650 --> 00:33:47.200
Now I will tell it immediately,
I want to use my card abroad--

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:52.460
which was one of the use
cases I told you about.

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:55.440
And the funny thing is
that within a minute

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:57.990
I will need this
one-time password.

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:01.170
And I had put my phone
away because I did not

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:02.310
want to be distracted.

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:06.240
But I have to be distracted.

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:06.750
OK.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:09.150
At first, we wanted
to use only text.

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:13.949
But quickly we learned that once
your customer is in a dialog,

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:15.780
please keep him there.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:18.830
And then, visual elements,
like you see here--

00:34:18.830 --> 00:34:20.280
it's called quick replies--

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:21.270
it helps a lot.

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:23.850
Now a debit card is a
more interesting case,

00:34:23.850 --> 00:34:25.800
because there it depends
on the destination,

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:28.440
if the card needs to
be activated as well.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.969
As you see, as well, Marie--

00:34:30.969 --> 00:34:32.100
her responses--

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.860
Are not, let's say, flat.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:38.386
There's a bit of joy,
there's a slight fun element,

00:34:38.386 --> 00:34:40.260
which is the personality
of a bot-- something

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:42.030
you need to think about.

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:45.540
Now I'm traveling to Peru.

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:49.469
And she will ask me if she
needs to activate my card.

00:34:49.469 --> 00:34:52.350
And I will tell her, yes,
that she needs to do so.

00:34:57.680 --> 00:35:01.690
And she has sent
me now a pin code--

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:08.195
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:35:08.195 --> 00:35:09.185
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:12.502 --> 00:35:13.210
TIM DANIELS: Yes.

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:13.709
Yeah.

00:35:13.709 --> 00:35:16.390
Those are small things
we still need to fix.

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:23.000
Sometimes, let's say, that the
conversations are not all yet

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:23.870
up to standard.

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:25.220
Aha.

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:27.420
And now comes an
important element,

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:30.590
which is nice in, for
example, Facebook Messenger.

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:32.330
This is a carousel.

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:34.460
Instead of asking
the customer which

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:37.490
card, or what are the
last digits, and so on,

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:40.910
we show the cards a person has.

00:35:40.910 --> 00:35:45.620
And then it becomes much
easier to choose one.

00:35:45.620 --> 00:35:51.990
Now I had to enlarge
the screen a bit.

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:53.900
So I'm missing here--

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:57.978
because there should be
extra cards available.

00:35:57.978 --> 00:36:00.300
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:02.020
TIM DANIELS: There they are.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:06.590
And so, as you can see, most
of the information on the cards

00:36:06.590 --> 00:36:07.780
has been masked.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:09.530
This is for security reasons.

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:11.420
Also, in the text
below, you will

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.600
see that we have applied
masking, because we do not

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.880
want Facebook to
know the numbers

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:19.220
of the cards of our customers.

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:20.720
They know that they
will have cards.

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:23.720
But that's not much information.

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:28.070
So I'm simply going to
tell Marie this one.

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:31.910
And then, like
Michiel already said,

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:34.030
this is quite
wonderful, of course.

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:36.710
Dashes, slashes-- you don't
need to worry about it.

00:36:36.710 --> 00:36:39.590
Natural language
will just make sure

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:44.170
that you get where
you need to be.

00:36:44.170 --> 00:36:45.540
Voila.

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:48.280
Now Marie will send--

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:51.500
she is now informing the
people at the back end

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.330
that they need to do something.

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:56.390
She has sent a
confirmation mail.

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:58.150
And that's about it.

00:36:58.150 --> 00:36:59.000
OK.

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.460
Now let's have a
look into her brain.

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:07.610
And at this point in time,
we have about 150 intents.

00:37:07.610 --> 00:37:10.910
So be prepared, when you're
going to build a real bot,

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:14.090
be prepared to have
a lot of intents.

00:37:14.090 --> 00:37:17.240
We have about 10
full-fledged use cases.

00:37:17.240 --> 00:37:19.740
I think half of the
intents are for that.

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:23.270
And then there's a lot of
stuff like small talk--

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:24.440
what are you wearing?

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:25.680
Where are you living?

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:26.770
How old are you?

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:28.310
Things people ask.

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:31.430
And you better have an answer
to that, otherwise they

00:37:31.430 --> 00:37:33.500
think Marie's boring,
which she is not.

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:40.340
To keep strictures,
the dialogues

00:37:40.340 --> 00:37:42.290
we have are all multi-intent.

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:46.580
And I will go quickly to the
use of brought one with it.

00:37:46.580 --> 00:37:49.580
So you see that we are
applying a naming convention.

00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:51.440
The name of the use
case is coming back.

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:53.090
And we are keeping
track of where

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:54.710
we are in the conversation.

00:37:54.710 --> 00:37:57.890
And to make that
work, you really

00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:01.430
need to get your contexts right.

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:04.190
So intents and entities
are very important.

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:07.250
But you will get nowhere
in a more advanced bot

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:08.900
without context.

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:12.620
So we are setting two contexts,
one for the overall use case,

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:15.920
and the second one to make sure
that the answer to the question

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:18.650
Marie is asking at
that point in time,

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:21.080
that that answer is
captured correctly.

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:24.110
So she's asking
for the card type.

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:28.880
So the outgoing context
is then about card types.

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:30.350
And then we have the intent--

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:36.950
if it's a credit card, then you
see this context coming back

00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:38.220
as an input context.

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:39.750
Context is very important.

00:38:39.750 --> 00:38:43.250
I am keeping, also,
an eye on the time.

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:46.520
So multi-intent
dialogues glued together

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:51.080
by context, which means that
the dialogue logic is fully

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:53.180
driven within API.AI.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:54.770
So no coding there.

00:38:54.770 --> 00:38:58.085
It's all configuration,
which we like.

00:38:58.085 --> 00:39:03.500
Now I will-- because
time is limited--

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:05.690
and I will not touch
upon Cloud Functions.

00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:07.790
This is a very rich topic.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:10.580
And we are around
to talk about it.

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:12.140
But it's better not
to talk about it

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:14.780
than to talk too
shortly about it.

00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:17.630
Now I will demonstrate
the last part, which

00:39:17.630 --> 00:39:20.580
is our conversation dashboard.

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:23.930
Therefore, I will
get quite impolite.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:28.747
So I will say, this
bot is terrible--

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:35.130
which I sometimes,
but seldomly, think.

00:39:38.540 --> 00:39:41.360
And normally, she is a bit sad.

00:39:44.306 --> 00:39:46.770
Ah.

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:47.634
Something--

00:39:47.634 --> 00:39:48.522
[BELLS]

00:39:48.522 --> 00:39:51.361
Oh, whoa.

00:39:51.361 --> 00:39:51.860
OK.

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:54.800
So now someone in
our contact center

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:58.760
has been notified that a
conversation is derailing.

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:01.460
And for us it is very
important that people--

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:03.860
even if the conversation--
the automatic conversation--

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:05.800
does not work out,
that they leave

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:07.220
Marie with a good feeling.

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.770
So we can step into
the conversation, which

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:12.890
is a second major role apart
from our authentication

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:14.300
of this middleman.

00:40:14.300 --> 00:40:19.760
So now you see here,
the complete trace

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:22.020
of the conversation--
like Facebook Messenger,

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.440
it stays available
for a long time.

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:29.000
And I can turn of Marie here.

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:38.230
And I can-- hi, Tim, from
the contact center here.

00:40:38.230 --> 00:40:40.930
Voila.

00:40:40.930 --> 00:40:43.460
And normally, voila.

00:40:43.460 --> 00:40:46.400
So human take over--

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:49.770
this is very, very important.

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:50.890
So if I--

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:53.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:53.290 --> 00:40:56.650
Thank you so much.

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:59.530
So if you ask me-- at
the last six months,

00:40:59.530 --> 00:41:03.250
you had a lot of fun
developing this bot.

00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:04.750
What are your main takeaways?

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:06.250
And I would say, one--

00:41:06.250 --> 00:41:08.230
make sure that you
have a clear scope.

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:11.800
And defend that against
the many stakeholders

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:14.020
you will get along your path.

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:20.410
Two-- make sure you master
the concept of contexts.

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:23.530
It's nontrivial, but
it's very powerful.

00:41:23.530 --> 00:41:26.950
And it will allow you to
make multi-intent dialogues.

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:30.940
Then, as a third element,
think about the personality

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:31.510
of your bot.

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:32.440
Make it explicit.

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:34.570
We worked with
marketing for that.

00:41:34.570 --> 00:41:37.310
And agree upon it.

00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:39.610
And then, the fourth
element is, make sure

00:41:39.610 --> 00:41:43.000
that your bot talks as quickly
as possible to other people

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:45.370
than those who are
building the bot.

00:41:45.370 --> 00:41:48.070
Because, each time
we did it, we were

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:51.430
amazed by the
creativity of people

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:55.930
to say or to ask for simple
things in 1,000 different ways.

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.660
So you hand over the bot.

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:00.550
Then you see people going wild.

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:04.090
And then you think, oh, I
never thought of that utterance

00:42:04.090 --> 00:42:05.210
and so on and so forth.

00:42:05.210 --> 00:42:08.650
So you need about
30 to 50 utterances

00:42:08.650 --> 00:42:13.690
for the entry intent, which is
the number we are striving for.

00:42:13.690 --> 00:42:15.760
That was number four.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:20.980
And number five is never
leave your bot alone.

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:23.290
And with this
concluding verse, I

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:26.170
would like to hand
the word over again

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.483
to Michiel for the wrap-up.

00:42:28.483 --> 00:42:30.898
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:35.702 --> 00:42:37.660
MICHIEL VANTHOOR: So,
thank you very much, Tim.

00:42:37.660 --> 00:42:42.340
Very cool and interesting to
see what you are building here.

00:42:42.340 --> 00:42:46.250
Just to wrap it up, we have
discussed some building blocks

00:42:46.250 --> 00:42:46.750
first.

00:42:46.750 --> 00:42:49.310
We were talking about
this user interface.

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:52.230
And there we said the
importance of being

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:54.130
cross-platform availability.

00:42:54.130 --> 00:42:57.070
So you have these native
integrations as well as

00:42:57.070 --> 00:42:59.560
these SDKs to integrate with.

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:01.800
API.AI-- very powerful.

00:43:01.800 --> 00:43:03.430
And on the fulfillment
side, you have

00:43:03.430 --> 00:43:07.180
a lot of options on the
Cloud platform as well.

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:10.510
Interesting to note, as well,
is that API.AI is for free right

00:43:10.510 --> 00:43:13.090
now, the native
integrators as well.

00:43:13.090 --> 00:43:15.400
And for example,
the Cloud Functions,

00:43:15.400 --> 00:43:18.230
the first one million calls
or something are for free.

00:43:18.230 --> 00:43:20.110
So it's really
easy to get going.

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:23.300
You don't need budget approvals
or something like that.

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:25.510
And then, when it works out,
like in the case of Tim,

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:28.810
you can easily
scale with demand.

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:31.930
One last thing I want to give
you before we wrap this up

00:43:31.930 --> 00:43:34.990
is that we've covered a lot
of technical talks here--

00:43:34.990 --> 00:43:37.150
demonstration and
technical insights.

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:40.370
But when you're dealing with
humans, like the marketing, the

00:43:40.370 --> 00:43:43.480
how you deal with people,
becomes very important as well.

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:45.700
While developing our
Google Assistant,

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:48.590
we have learned
lessons along the way.

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:50.680
And we're also sharing
this with the community.

00:43:50.680 --> 00:43:53.890
So also put some
considerable amount

00:43:53.890 --> 00:43:57.530
of effort in this
interaction with humans.

00:43:57.530 --> 00:43:59.230
So, basically, that's it.

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:01.090
I hope it was a very
interesting session.

00:44:01.090 --> 00:44:03.310
We did the basic
run-through and then

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:06.980
the expert experience of someone
running it into production.

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:08.770
So I want to thank Tim again.

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:12.710
I want to thank you for your
attention and your energy.

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:14.770
And I hope you have a
very nice rest of the day.

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:15.580
Thank you.

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:17.430
[APPLAUSE]

